The paradox of Eliezer Yudkowsky is that he's an important thinker but also, as some argue, a terrible AI researcher with poor intuition on the subject.

@alexandrosM quotes an unbelievable Yudkowsky statement, where he not only dismisses neural networks as unlikely to achieve breakthroughs but also believes no AI could appear human-like in conversation without a catastrophic event soon after.

The linked article discusses how the founder of the Machine Intelligence Research Institute thinks humanity is already doomed to a catastrophic AI apocalypse, raising concerns about the timing and severity of AI risks.

But remember, we are in mirror-world: the AI that detects the AI is trained on human writing, so, really, where are we? In the dark.

Extraordinary.

@ObserverUK: "My writing process is unusual," says prize-winning author accused of being AI. Was The Serpent in the Grove written by a bot? How real is the author himself? Now that suspicion has crept into the literary world, it will be difficult to eradicate.

